markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Expanded Prompt Library

#### On the last level were just a few examples of prompts you could use to improve your teaching practice. Here are even more.

#### 👉 Click on the title to reveal the full prompt.


>#### **💬 Quick Reflection:** As you review, try to pick one of these that you could see yourself using.

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Planning and Preparation</strong>: Creating a Lesson Hook</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an experienced teacher and instructional designer.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I am planning a lesson on <strong>[topic]</strong>.
My learning goal for this lesson is:
<strong>[learning goal or standard]</strong>
I want to create a strong opening or “hook” that captures student interest and helps connect the lesson to prior knowledge.
Please generate:
- 3 lesson hook ideas appropriate for this topic and grade level
- A brief explanation of why each hook would work
- 1 suggestion for adapting each hook for students who may need additional support
Keep the hooks realistic for a <strong>[class length]</strong> class period and appropriate for my classroom context:
<strong>[school/classroom context]</strong>
</div>
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Planning and Preparation</strong>: Generating Discussion Questions</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an experienced teacher helping me prepare for instruction.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and my students are learning about <strong>[topic/text/concept]</strong>.
I need discussion questions that support this learning goal:
<strong>[learning goal or standard]</strong>
Please create:
- 3 questions that check understanding
- 3 questions that promote deeper thinking or discussion
- 2 follow-up prompts I can use if students get stuck
Make the questions appropriate for <strong>[grade level]</strong> students and aligned to this classroom context:
<strong>[relevant student or lesson context]</strong>
</div>
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Planning and Preparation</strong>: Building a Short Formative Check</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an experienced teacher and assessment designer.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I need a short formative check for a lesson on <strong>[topic]</strong>.
My learning target is:
<strong>[learning target or standard]</strong>
Please create:
- 3 quick questions or tasks to check student understanding
- A brief answer key or sample responses
- 1 suggestion for how I could use the results to adjust instruction
Keep the formative check short enough to use in <strong>[time available]</strong> and appropriate for my students:
<strong>[classroom context]</strong>
</div>
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Planning and Preparation</strong>: Differentiating an assessment</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an experienced teacher and instructional designer.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I am creating a formative assessment on <strong>[topic/standard]</strong>.
Here is my original assessment:
<strong>[Paste or describe the assessment]</strong>
My goal is to differentiate this assessment so all students can access the same learning goal:
<strong>[learning objective/standard]</strong>
Please create:
- A scaffolded version (for students who need support)
- A grade-level version
- An extension version (for advanced learners)
For each version:
- Keep it aligned to the same learning objective
- Adjust language, structure, and supports as needed
- Include clear student directions
- Maintain meaningful thinking (not just simplification)
Also include:
- A short explanation of how each version supports different learners
- Suggested supports (sentence starters, vocabulary, graphic organizers, etc.)
</details>


<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Communication and Materials Creation</strong>: Writing a Family Newsletter Blurb</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an experienced teacher helping draft family communication.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject or classroom]</strong>, and I need a short newsletter blurb for families about:
<strong>[topic, unit, event, or update]</strong>
Please write a short message that:
- Explains what students are learning or doing
- Uses a warm, clear, family-friendly tone
- Includes any important next steps or reminders
Please include these details:
<strong>[important details to include]</strong>
Keep it concise and easy to understand for families.
</div>
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Communication and Materials Creation</strong>: Revising Student Directions</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an experienced teacher helping improve classroom materials.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I want to revise directions for this student task:
<strong>[Paste current directions]</strong>
My goal is to make the directions clearer and easier for students to follow without lowering rigor.
Please:
- Rewrite the directions for clarity
- Break complex steps into simpler parts if needed
- Suggest any headings, bullets, or formatting that would help students follow the task
- Point out any parts that may confuse students
Keep the directions appropriate for <strong>[grade level]</strong> students and aligned to this task:
<strong>[task context]</strong>
</div>
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Communication and Materials Creation</strong>: Creating a Classroom Handout</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an experienced teacher helping create classroom materials.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I need a simple student-facing handout about:
<strong>[topic/skill/process]</strong>
The handout should help students:
<strong>[purpose of the handout]</strong>
Please create a draft handout that includes:
- A clear title
- A short explanation in student-friendly language
- Key points or steps students should remember
- An example, if helpful
Keep it concise, clear, and appropriate for <strong>[grade level]</strong> students in this classroom context:
<strong>[classroom context]</strong>
</div>
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Communication and Materials Creation</strong>: Writing a parent email</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an experienced teacher helping draft communication with families.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I need to write an email to a parent/guardian about:
<strong>[purpose of the email]</strong>
Here are the key points to include:
- <strong>[Key point 1]</strong>
- <strong>[Key point 2]</strong>
- <strong>[Key point 3]</strong>
Additional context (tone, relationship, sensitivity, etc.):
<strong>[optional context]</strong>
Please write an email that:
- Is clear, concise, and easy to understand
- Uses a respectful, supportive, and professional tone
- Reflects collaboration with families
Then provide:
- 1–2 alternative ways to phrase key parts of the message
- Suggestions to improve clarity or tone if needed
</div>
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Reflection and Improvement</strong>: Analyzing a Student Misconception</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an instructional coach helping me reflect on student understanding.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and during a lesson on <strong>[topic]</strong>, I noticed this common misconception:
<strong>[describe the misconception]</strong>
Please help me think through:
- Possible reasons students may be confused
- What in my instruction may have contributed to the misunderstanding
- 2 or 3 strategies I could try next to address it
Keep your feedback practical and appropriate for this classroom context:
<strong>[classroom context]</strong>
</div>
</details>


<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Reflection and Improvement</strong>: Improving a Classroom Task</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an instructional coach providing feedback on task design.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I used this classroom task:
<strong>[Paste or describe the task]</strong>
After using it, I noticed:
<strong>[what students did, where they struggled, or what felt off]</strong>
Please give feedback on:
- Whether the task was aligned to the learning goal
- Whether the directions and structure were clear
- How I could improve the task for next time
Please suggest 2 specific revisions I could make while keeping the same learning goal:
<strong>[learning goal or standard]</strong>
</div>
</details>


<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Reflection and Improvement</strong>: Planning the Next Step After a Lesson</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an experienced instructional coach helping me plan next steps.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I just taught a lesson on <strong>[topic]</strong>.
Here is what I observed:
<strong>[student responses, engagement, strengths, struggles, assessment results]</strong>
Based on this, please help me:
- Identify what students seem to understand
- Identify what still needs reinforcement
- Suggest 2 next-step instructional moves for my next class
Keep your suggestions realistic for <strong>[class length]</strong> and aligned to this learning goal:
<strong>[learning goal or standard]</strong>
</div>
</details>


<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
<summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Reflection and Improvement</strong>: Getting feedback on a lesson plan</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">You are an instructional coach providing actionable feedback.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I want feedback on a lesson about:
<strong>[lesson topic]</strong>
Here is my lesson plan:
<strong>[Paste or describe the lesson]</strong>
My goals for the lesson:
- <strong>[Goal 1]</strong>
- <strong>[Goal 2]</strong>
After teaching it, I noticed:
<strong>[what worked / what didn’t / student challenges]</strong>
Please give feedback on:
- Alignment to the learning goals
- Clarity of instructions
- Level of rigor and student thinking
- Opportunities for differentiation
Then suggest:
- Specific improvements I could make
- 1–2 alternative strategies to address student challenges
Keep feedback practical and focused on changes I can realistically implement.
</div>
</details>


<div style="clear:both;"></div>


MARKDOWN
